Using **@ollama-fast** context: here is a correct **concurrency-limited URL fetcher** in TypeScript. (The local model draft had ordering and completion bugs; this version fixes those.)

## `fetchUrlsWithConcurrency`

Uses a **worker pool**: up to `concurrencyNumber` workers run at once; when one finishes, it picks the next URL until the list is done. Results stay in the **same order as `urls`**.

```typescript
export type FetchResult = {
  url: string;
  ok: boolean;
  status: number;
  body: string;
};

/**
 * Fetches URLs with a fixed concurrency limit.
 * When one fetch completes, the next URL starts until all are done.
 * Results are returned in the same order as `urls`.
 */
export async function fetchUrlsWithConcurrency(
  urls: string[],
  concurrencyNumber: number
): Promise<FetchResult[]> {
  if (concurrencyNumber < 1) {
    throw new Error("concurrencyNumber must be at least 1");
  }
  if (urls.length === 0) return [];

  const results: FetchResult[] = new Array(urls.length);
  let nextIndex = 0;

  async function worker(): Promise<void> {
    while (true) {
      const index = nextIndex++;
      if (index >= urls.length) break;

      const url = urls[index];
      const response = await fetch(url);
      const body = await response.text();

      results[index] = {
        url,
        ok: response.ok,
        status: response.status,
        body,
      };
    }
  }

  const workerCount = Math.min(concurrencyNumber, urls.length);
  await Promise.all(
    Array.from({ length: workerCount }, () => worker())
  );

  return results;
}
```

### How it works

1. Pre-allocate `results` so slot `i` always matches `urls[i]`.
2. Shared `nextIndex` — each worker takes the next URL atomically (single-threaded JS, so `nextIndex++` is safe).
3. Start `min(concurrencyNumber, urls.length)` workers.
4. Each worker loops: fetch → store at `results[index]` → take another URL until none remain.
5. `Promise.all` waits until all workers are idle, then return.

### Example

```typescript
const urls = [
  "https://httpbin.org/delay/2",
  "https://httpbin.org/delay/1",
  "https://httpbin.org/get",
  "https://httpbin.org/uuid",
];

const results = await fetchUrlsWithConcurrency(urls, 2);
console.log(results.map((r) => ({ url: r.url, status: r.status })));
```

With `concurrencyNumber: 2`, at most two requests run at a time; when one finishes, the next URL starts.

---

## Variants

**Return `Response` objects only** (no body read):

```typescript
const results: Response[] = new Array(urls.length);
// ... in worker:
results[index] = await fetch(url);
```

**JSON instead of text:**

```typescript
const data = await response.json();
results[index] = { url, ok: response.ok, status: response.status, data };
```

**Don't fail the whole batch on one error** (`Promise.allSettled` style per URL):

```typescript
try {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  results[index] = { url, ok: response.ok, status: response.status, body: await response.text() };
} catch (error) {
  results[index] = {
    url,
    ok: false,
    status: 0,
    body: "",
    error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),
  };
}
```

---

## Prerequisites

- **Node 18+** (built-in `fetch`) or a polyfill
- **Ollama** only matters if you want the agent to call MCP; this function runs in your app with `fetch`

